    rdfs:label "Evidence for Dye Woods Logwood used in Hat-Making, Brushes, Upholstery & Turnery: P. L. Simmonds, The Commercial Products of the Vegetable Kingdom (1854; Project Gutenberg #15191), section LOGWOOD"@en ;
    <ontology/quoteText/#resource> "LOGWOOD: The logwood of commerce is the red heart wood, or duramen, of a fine lofty growing tree (Haematroxylon Campechianum), growing in Campeachy and the bay of Honduras, and which is also now common in the woods of Jamaica and St. Domingo. It is principally imported as a dye wood, cut into short lengths. We chip, grind, and pack it into casks and bags, re..."@en ;
    <ontology/retrievalMode/#resource> "direct_sentence" .
